WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.080
The loss is on the left one. Why is the right one in danger too?

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:13.941
Cross margin shares margin across positions, within what the rules allow.

00:00:13.941 --> 00:00:16.382
Here, say one margin pool holds 1,000.

00:00:16.382 --> 00:00:21.330
Several positions may depend on it, not just on what each was first assigned.

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:42.974
One losing position can eat the buffer other positions rely on.

00:00:42.974 --> 00:00:45.634
Profits, losses, margin held for open orders,

00:00:45.634 --> 00:00:49.890
and the value of your collateral can all affect the same account's risk.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:19.370
So cross margin is harder to liquidate, which makes it safer?

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:22.731
Extra money can delay when one position hits risk handling.

00:01:22.731 --> 00:01:26.000
But it also puts more of your money on the line for the losses.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:50.893
Cross margin doesn't let a platform automatically use all your bank cards or personal wallets.

00:01:50.893 --> 00:01:54.103
Which accounts and collateral count depends on the product rules.

00:01:54.103 --> 00:01:57.560
Complex collateral can also come with haircuts and its own price risk.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:14.890
Not necessarily. It depends on the shared pool and the account rules.

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:18.320
Look at the whole pool, not just one position's box.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:29.803
The price you watch hasn't hit the line, but the alarm is ringing.

00:02:29.803 --> 00:02:32.010
Next: who presses the liquidation button.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.570
Understand first, then act.
